WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.460
Here are a hundred newborn females, all born in the same season.

2
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.880
Only ten of them will live to age three.

3
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.120
Can this group still more than replace itself?

4
00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:17.690
A group born together is called a cohort.

5
00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:22.850
Follow it year by year, and at each age count how many are still alive.

6
00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:25.610
Those that die fade to grey.

7
00:00:26.683 --> 00:00:28.983
Divide each count by the starting hundred.

8
00:00:30.223 --> 00:00:34.803
That fraction is l_x, the share of the cohort still alive at age x.

9
00:00:36.023 --> 00:00:37.603
Here it falls from 1 to 0.1.

10
00:00:38.683 --> 00:00:40.643
Survivors also give birth.

11
00:00:42.143 --> 00:00:46.463
Life tables like this usually count females only, so the births we count are

12
00:00:46.463 --> 00:00:47.463
daughters.

13
00:00:47.967 --> 00:00:53.127
At each age, m_x is the average number of daughters born per living female.

14
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:58.447
Here the averages are 0 in the first year, then 1, 2, and 1.

15
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:04.640
Now the key step. A newborn female may not live to a given age.

16
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.940
So her daughters there count only as far as survival allows.

17
00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:13.500
Multiply the share still alive by the daughters each one has.

18
00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:18.640
That is what an average newborn female can expect at that age.

19
00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.010
Add those pieces across her whole life.

20
00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:27.550
The total is R0, the daughters left by an average newborn female.

21
00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:30.410
Here it comes to 1.7.

22
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.020
The dashed line marks one.

23
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.680
At one, each female just replaces herself.

24
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.700
Above it, each generation is larger than the last.

25
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.200
Below it, each is smaller.

26
00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:47.200
So yes, this cohort more than replaces itself.

27
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:50.770
Now give the cohort a hard first winter.

28
00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:54.510
Only half as many females reach age one.

29
00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:56.810
What happens to R0?

30
00:02:03.417 --> 00:02:07.057
Keep every later survival chance and birth rate the same.

31
00:02:08.377 --> 00:02:12.497
Every female alive at two or three first had to survive that winter.

32
00:02:13.797 --> 00:02:17.697
So the share alive halves at every later age, not just at age one.

33
00:02:18.717 --> 00:02:23.437
Every piece of R0 halves, so R0 halves too, to below the line.

34
00:02:24.717 --> 00:02:28.517
Survival to any age is the product of every year before it.

35
00:02:29.537 --> 00:02:32.337
So a loss early in life cuts every later birth.

36
00:02:33.367 --> 00:02:38.847
Two cautions. R0 is counted per generation, not per year.

37
00:02:40.087 --> 00:02:43.087
A yearly rate also needs the length of a generation.

38
00:02:44.247 --> 00:02:49.967
And R0 predicts long-run growth only if the rates stay fixed, and nobody arrives or

39
00:02:49.967 --> 00:02:50.287
leaves.
